From 1f6530e564b6669a85c52dcdaba9563088e20363 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:01:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] drm/gma500: Publish the Cedarview SGX as an auxiliary
 device

The GMA3600/3650 (Cedarview) GPU is a PowerVR SGX545 behind the same PCI
function and register BAR as the display engine gma500 drives. gma500 has
no use for the SGX beyond its own MMU setup, so give it to a driver of its
own: publish an auxiliary device, "gma500_gfx.sgx", carrying the mapped SGX
register window, which SGX core it is, and a hook that routes the SGX's
interrupt to that driver.

While a handler is registered, gma500 calls it for the SGX interrupt in
place of its own handling, and writes no SGX register: not the event
enables at interrupt (re)install, nor the SGX MMU's directory and flush
bits when it pins framebuffers. Its own page tables still track what it
maps, the hardware just never sees them while the SGX has another owner.
Unbinding the SGX driver hands everything back.

The auxiliary device's parent is the GPU's PCI device, so runtime PM on
the SGX side keeps gma500, and with it the graphics power island, awake.

Each chip's psb_ops says whether its SGX is published, and as which core.
Only Cedarview's is filled in; Poulsbo and Oaktrail leave it NULL and are
unchanged until an SGX driver has run on them.
